<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="description" content="A semantic HTML5 example demonstrating accessibility, SEO, and proper document structure without CSS or JavaScript.">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Semantic HTML5 Page Example</title>
</head>

<body>

    <header>
        <h1>Semantic HTML5 Best Practices</h1>
        <p>A structured, accessible, and search-engine-friendly example page.</p>
    </header>

    <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <ul>
            <li><a href="#introduction">Introduction</a></li>
            <li><a href="#structure">Page Structure</a></li>
            <li><a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a></li>
            <li><a href="#seo">SEO Considerations</a></li>
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <main id="main-content">
        
        <section id="introduction">
            <h2>Introduction</h2>
            <p>
                This page demonstrates how to build a well-structured HTML5 document
                using semantic elements that benefit users, assistive technologies,
                and search engines.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="structure">
            <h2>Logical Page Structure</h2>

            <article>
                <h3>Using Semantic Elements</h3>
                <p>
                    Semantic elements such as <code>header</code>, <code>nav</code>,
                    <code>main</code>, <code>section</code>, and <code>footer</code>
                    clearly describe the purpose of each part of the page.
                </p>
            </article>

            <article>
                <h3>Content Hierarchy</h3>
                <p>
                    Headings are used in a logical order, starting with one
                    <code>h1</code>, followed by <code>h2</code> and <code>h3</code>
                    elements. This improves readability and allows search engines
                    to understand content relationships.
                </p>
            </article>
        </section>

        <section id="accessibility">
            <h2>Accessibility Features</h2>
            <p>
                HTML5 landmarks help screen readers identify major regions of the page.
                Descriptive link text and navigation labels improve keyboard and
                assistive technology navigation.
            </p>

            <aside>
                <h3>Why Accessibility Matters</h3>
                <p>
                    Accessible markup ensures that all users, including those using
                    screen readers or keyboard navigation, can access and understand
                    your content.
                </p>
            </aside>
        </section>

        <section id="seo">
            <h2>SEO-Friendly Markup</h2>
            <p>
                Search engines rely on semantic structure and meaningful text.
                Proper use of headings, descriptive paragraphs, and metadata
                improves discoverability and indexing.
            </p>
        </section>

    </main>

    <footer>
        <p>
            &copy; 2025 Semantic Web Example. All rights reserved.
        </p>
        <address>
            Written by a web development mentor with over a decade of experience.
        </address>
    </footer>

</body>
</html>
